+# AprilgridDetector
+
+Robust, real-time AprilGrid detection in Rust, with Python bindings.
+
+Detects 36h11 AprilTags, groups them into boards, and recovers 6-DoF pose from
+camera intrinsics. Written from scratch (no OpenCV or AprilTag dependency in
+the core) and validated against a physically-based simulator.
+
+## Status
+
+| Metric (1920x1080, 8-core x86-64) | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Throughput, `decimate=2` | **34.1 fps** |
+| Corner accuracy | **0.25 px RMSE** |
+| Rotation error | 0.36 deg mean |
+| Translation error | 0.89 mm mean |
+| Detection rate, easy / normal / hard | 100% / 86.9% / 67.2% |
+| False positives | **0** across all datasets |
+| Tests | 116 Rust, 13 Python binding |
+| Platforms | Linux x86-64, Windows x86-64 (MSVC) |
+
+Detection rate is measured only against tags that are genuinely visible and
+unoccluded. Remaining misses are concentrated below 20 px apparent tag size
+and above 4 px of motion blur.
+
+The full suite runs on both Linux and Windows. Accuracy agrees across the two:
+same size-response curve, 0.23 px corner RMSE and 0.32 deg rotation error on a
+Windows-generated dataset, with zero false positives on both.
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+crates/aprilgrid/       core detector (no_std-friendly deps: nalgebra, rayon)
+crates/aprilgrid-py/    pyo3 bindings -> aprilgrid_detector module
+crates/aprilgrid-cli/   agdet command-line tool
+tools/                  simulator, evaluator, diagnostics (Python + cv2)
+```
+
+## Building
+
+Dependencies are vendored into `vendor/`, so every build works offline. Keep
+`--offline` on the command line to make that explicit.
+
+```bash
+cargo build --workspace --release --offline
+cargo test -p aprilgrid --offline
+```
+
+### Windows
+
+If Rust was installed without adding itself to `PATH`, prepend the toolchain
+for the session first:
+
+```powershell
+$env:Path = "C:\Program Files\Rust stable MSVC 1.97\bin;$env:Path"
+cargo build --workspace --release --offline
+```
+
+Two PowerShell caveats when driving these builds from a script or an agent:
+piping `cargo` into another cmdlet makes PowerShell report exit code 1 even on
+success (check `$LASTEXITCODE` after `cargo ... *>$null` instead), and
+multi-line commands pasted into a terminal may execute only the first line.
+
+## Install (Python)
+
+```bash
+maturin build --release --out dist --manifest-path crates/aprilgrid-py/Cargo.toml
+pip install dist/aprilgrid_detector-*.whl
+```
+
+For local development: `maturin develop --release`.
+
+Verify the install with `python tools/test_binding.py` (13 tests, no pytest
+required).
+
+## Python usage
+
+### Detect tags
+
+```python
+import cv2
+import aprilgrid_detector as agd
+
+img = cv2.imread("frame.png", cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE)
+
+detector = agd.Detector()             # or agd.Detector.realtime_hd()
+for d in detector.detect(img):
+    print(d.id, d.corners, d.decision_margin)
+```
+
+The image must be a 2-D, C-contiguous `uint8` array. Convert colour with
+`cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)` and non-contiguous views with
+`numpy.ascontiguousarray(img)`.
+
+`corners` are ordered as printed: top-left, top-right, bottom-right,
+bottom-left — matching `cv2.aruco.GridBoard`. Pixel centres lie on integer
+coordinates, so pixel `i` spans `[i-0.5, i+0.5]` (OpenCV convention).
+
+### Detect boards and estimate pose
+
+```python
+layout = agd.BoardLayout(tags_x=4, tags_y=1,
+                         tag_size=0.020, tag_separation=0.0075)
+camera = agd.Camera(fx=1400, fy=1400, cx=959.5, cy=539.5,
+                    distortion=(-0.18, 0.04, 0.0004, -0.0003, -0.002))
+
+for board in detector.detect_boards(img, layout, camera):
+    p = board.pose
+    print(board.board_id, board.coverage, p.translation, p.rvec,
+          p.reprojection_rmse)
+```
+
+Pass `camera=None` to group tags into boards without estimating pose.
+Reuse intrinsics straight from calibration with
+`agd.Camera.from_matrix(K, dist)`.
+
+Tags map to boards by id: `board_id = tag_id // (tags_x * tags_y)`.
+
+### Tuning
+
+```python
+agd.Detector(
+    decimate=1,             # search at 1/N resolution; 2 is the realtime setting
+    min_contrast=12,        # local contrast floor, 0-255
+    min_cluster_size=24,    # smallest boundary cluster
+    refine_edges=True,      # sub-pixel edge refinement
+    parallel=True,
+    min_side_px=None,       # smallest accepted quad side
+    min_decision_margin=None,
+)
+```
+
+`decimate=2` searches at half resolution but always measures corners and
+decodes the payload at full resolution, so it costs roughly 0.03 px of corner
+accuracy while running ~4.5x faster. Use it whenever tags exceed about 40 px.
+
+The GIL is released during detection, so frames can be processed from a
+thread pool.
+
+## Rust usage
+
+```rust
+use aprilgrid::detector::Detector;
+use aprilgrid::gray::GrayImage;
+
+let detector = Detector::t36h11();
+let detections = detector.detect(&image);
+```
+
+## Command line
+
+```bash
+cargo build -p aprilgrid-cli --release --offline   # -> target/release/agdet
+agdet frame.png
+agdet --intrinsics 1400,1400,959.5,539.5 --json frames/*.png
+```
+
+Text output lists each tag with its hamming distance, decision margin and
+centre, then one line per board with coverage, pose and reprojection RMSE.
+`--json` emits a machine-readable array instead. Run `agdet --help` for the
+full option list (board geometry, distortion, decimation, and detector
+thresholds).
+
+The argument parser is hand-rolled — `clap` is not vendored — so it accepts
+only the exact spellings in `--help`. Bad input exits non-zero with a message.
+
+## Simulator and evaluation
+
+The simulator renders an AprilGrid under a sampled pose through a full
+radial-tangential lens model, then applies motion blur, defocus, sensor noise,
+vignetting, illumination gradients, exposure error, occlusion, and JPEG
+artefacts. It emits exact ground truth alongside the images.
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/simulate.py --out data/sim_normal --count 60 --seed 7 --preset normal
+python3 tools/evaluate.py --data data/sim_normal --decimate 2
+python3 tools/diagnose.py --data data/sim_normal
+```
+
+- `simulate.py` — dataset generation (`--preset easy|normal|hard`)
+- `evaluate.py` — detection rate, corner/pose error, speed, size breakdown
+- `diagnose.py` — cross-tabulates misses against blur and tag size to
+  attribute failures
+- `sweep.py` — parameter grid search (run from inside `tools/`)
+- `shadow_test.py` — partial cast shadows (see below)
+
+Profile the pipeline stage by stage with:
+
+```bash
+cargo run --release --example profile -p aprilgrid -- <images>... --decimate 2
+```
+
+### Shadow test
+
+A shadow edge crossing a tag puts a step of light *inside* it, which the
+frame-wide illumination ramp never does. `shadow_test.py` casts controlled
+shadows on the printed sheet and the table under it, sweeping shape (`edge`
+half-plane, 1.5-cell `bar`), penumbra (hard, 1 cell, 4 cells) and strength.
+Tags are scored as *partial* (the edge crosses the tag or its one-cell white
+ring), *umbra* (fully shadowed) or *lit*. Every config sees the same noise, so
+the only difference between configs is the shadow.
+
+```bash
+python tools/shadow_test.py [--distance 0.6] [--decimate 2] [--examples DIR]
+python tools/test_shadow_model.py      # unit tests for the shadow renderer
+```
+
+Partial-shadow detection rate, 67 px tags, `decimate=1`:
+
+| Strength | edge, hard | edge, 1 cell | edge, 4 cell | bar, hard | bar, 4 cell |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 0.30 | 100% | 100% | 100% | 100% | 100% |
+| 0.50 | 48% | 97% | 100% | 28% | 100% |
+| 0.70 | 6% | 6% | 99% | 11% | 100% |
+| 0.95 | 6% | 5% | 14% | 11% | 27% |
+
+Fully shadowed and lit tags stay at 100%, with no false positives. The cliff
+sits at strength 0.5 because the threshold is the midpoint of local min and
+max: once shadowed white falls below half of lit white, it binarises as black
+and the outline breaks. Only a penumbra wider than the 12 px threshold window
+postpones it.
+
+Pose error (mean over frames that still yield a pose; baseline 0.07 mm /
+0.05 deg). The *control* re-fits the pose from the unshadowed corners of the
+same surviving tags, so shadow minus control is the corner bias alone:
+
+| Case | Board lost | Shadow mm / deg | Control mm / deg |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| strength 0.3, or soft and <= 0.5 | 0% | 0.08 / 0.06 | 0.07 / 0.05 |
+| hard edge, 0.5 | 1% | 0.23 / 0.37 | 0.17 / 0.29 |
+| hard edge, 0.7-0.85 | 25% | 0.26 / 0.23 | 0.24 / 0.23 |
+| any edge, 0.95 | 25% | 0.90 / 0.68 | 0.24 / 0.23 |
+
+Losing tags dominates: a shadow along the 4x1 board splits every tag and the
+pose is gone, and fits from one surviving tag reach 3.6 mm / 2.1 deg with or
+without the shadow. Corner bias matters at two points only: strength 0.5
+with a hard edge, where the edge sits beside the outline and pulls the
+sub-pixel fit, and 0.95, where umbra tags drop to ~13 grey levels of
+contrast and their corners err by 0.75 px.
+
+## Robot-arm tests
+
+`tools/robotlab/` puts the detector on an eye-in-hand rig: an arm moves a
+camera around a fixed AprilGrid and the tests ask whether the recovered pose is
+usable for motion. The same three tests run against a simulator or against real
+hardware — `--backend` picks `configs/<name>.yaml`, and nothing else changes.
+
+```bash
+python tools/robotlab/run.py transforms                    # pose maths self-check
+python tools/robotlab/run.py probe                         # camera + detector smoke test
+python tools/robotlab/run.py reproducibility --auto
+python tools/robotlab/run.py consistency --auto
+python tools/robotlab/run.py touch --show                  # interactive; --auto to skip prompts
+
+python tools/robotlab/run.py probe --backend techman --arm # real hardware
+python tools/robotlab/run.py reproducibility --backend techman
+```
+
+`--config other.yaml` overrides only the keys it mentions.
+
+Configs are YAML (`PyYAML` required); keys whose name starts with `_` are
+ignored by the loader, which is how the files park alternative values you can
+swap in by renaming.
+
+| Test | Question | Simulation result (30 viewpoints, `normal` degradations) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Reproducibility | Does `T_base_grid` stay put as the camera moves? | 0.37 mm rms / 0.23 deg rms spread |
+| Consistency | Is the recovered transform between two grids the known one? | 0.20 mm / 0.19 deg mean error |
+| Touch | Can a tool tip reach an object located only through the grid? | 0.37 mm mean miss |
+
+These are one seed's worth of a fairly wide distribution, not tight bounds:
+across seeds 7-101 the reproducibility spread ranges 0.29-0.52 mm and the touch
+miss 0.13-0.54 mm. Compare runs at several seeds before reading anything into a
+change. The simulator's renderer and its viewpoint sampler share one generator,
+so capturing one extra image reorders every viewpoint that follows.
+
+These report measurements rather than asserting thresholds. The two things
+they do assert are the preconditions: the grid does not move during a run, and
+the arm has a clear view of it at the start. That the numbers mean anything is
+checked by breaking them on purpose — offsetting `hand_eye.T_ee_cam_true` by
+5 mm widens the reproducibility spread from 0.37 mm to 2.01 mm.
+
+### Running against a real arm
+
+Only two things differ on hardware, and both are forced by the absence of
+ground truth:
+
+- **Every motion needs a keypress.** `--auto` is refused outright on a real
+  backend; there is no flag to override it.
+- **The touch miss is measured by the operator**, who types it in each round.
+  Simulation reads it off ground truth instead.
+
+Everything else — viewpoint planning, the pose chain, the statistics — is the
+same code, because viewpoints are planned from the *estimated* grid pose and
+the printed geometry, never from truth.
+
+Each run begins at `robot.home_pose` and **measures** the grid from there; that
+detection is what the first viewing pose is aimed at. `scene.T_base_grid` is
+only a fallback for when the grid is not in frame from home, so jogging the arm
+to a pose that sees the grid matters far more than typing an accurate grid pose
+into the config.
+
+Start with `run.py probe --backend techman`. It talks only to the camera, never
+moves the arm, and prints the intrinsics block matching the camera's current
+image size and focus, ready to paste into the config. It refuses to proceed if
+the captured image size disagrees with the config, because the intrinsics would
+then be silently wrong. Add `--arm` to query the controller read-only.
+
+`drivers/techman.py` covers a Techman TM arm over gRPC. It converts the
+controller's millimetres and degrees to metres and radians at the boundary, and
+re-reads the pose after every move — a motion graph reporting *done* is not the
+same as the arm having *arrived*, and the difference would otherwise be
+recorded as a good sample.
+
+### Porting to another arm
+
+`RobotArm` in `tools/robotlab/core/robot.py` is abstract — a real arm needs only
+`get_pose()` and `move_pose()`, both taking `[x, y, z, rx, ry, rz]` in metres
+and radians, plus the hand-eye transform `T_ee_cam`. Camera pose, tool pose
+and relative motion are derived from those. Pair it with a `CameraSource` and a
+`Backend`, add a config naming it, and the tests work unchanged. The Euler
+convention (fixed-axis XYZ, i.e. `R = Rz·Ry·Rx`) is defined in exactly two
+functions in `tools/robotlab/core/transforms.py` and is the only place to
+change it.
+
+## Conventions
+
+These are locked and covered by tests; violating them breaks accuracy silently.
+
+1. Pixel centres at integer coordinates; pixel `i` covers `[i-0.5, i+0.5]`.
+2. Corner order: top-left, top-right, bottom-right, bottom-left (as printed).
+3. Tag code bit `35 - (row*6 + col)`, top-left origin, set bit = white cell.
+4. Board object frame: origin at the top-left corner of the top-left tag,
+   x right, y down, z = 0, in metres.
+
